 महोदय, हमारे �वद्वान �मत्र ने बड़ा मौ�लक प्रश्न उठाया है, \nऋण क्रां�त योजना के अन्तगर्त एक लाख तीस हजार से अ�धक प्रस्ताव अनुमो�दत �कए गए ह�। कु ल \nबया�लस सौ करोड़ रुपए के �लए है। इसी के तहत एक करोड़ रुपए तक हमने क्रे � ड ट गारंट� योजना म� \n�कया है। क्रे � ड ट गारंट� योजना म� छोटे लघु उद्योग सैक्टर के �लए �सडबी को सात हजार करोड़ रुपया \nहमने �दया है। �फर उ स के बाद दो हजार करोड़ रुपया और �दया गया। के व ल इस सैक्टर को मजबूत \nकरने के �लए �सडबी के आधार पर हम काम कर रहे ह�। आपने जो दूसरा प्रश्न पूछा है, उ स के �लए \n�डटे ल म� आपको �लख कर भेज दूंगा। \nSHRI KUMAR DEEPAK DAS: Sir, I would like to know from the hon. \nMinister whether they have taken any action against the banks, because \nbanks are not cooperating with the SMEs. The banks have to manage the \nentrepreneurs. The SMEs want to have marketing management services and \nbank assistance, but banks are not cooperating with them. Has the \nGovernment taken any action against those banks? It not, are they \ncontemlating to take any action against them? \nश्री महावीर प्रसाद: आदरणीय सभाप�त जी, माननीय सदस्य ने ब�क� के �खलाफ कायर्वाह� करने के \nबारे म� सवाल पूछा है। म� आ प के माध्यम से उनसे आग्रह क रूं ग ा �क �कसी भी ब�क के �खलाफ या ब�क� \nक� जो ग�त�व�धयां ह�, उनक े �खलाफ वह �लखकर द�। अभी तक म�ने इसम� कोई कायर्वाह� नह�ं क� है, \nचूं�क कहने के �लए कह �दया जाता है, अगर �लखकर द� �क अमुक ब�क ने अमुक व्यिक्त को यह ऋण \nनह�ं �दया है, ...(व्यवधान)... \nश्री �शवानन्द �तवार�: मंत्री जी, इ स के बारे म� अहलुवा�लया जी ने अभी आपको बताया है। \n...(व्यवधान)... \nश्री महावीर प्रसाद: उन्ह�ने अभी बताया तो है, ले�कन �तवार� जी, इ स के बारे म� �लखकर द�िजए। \nहम तो जांच कराने के �लए तैयार ह�, परन्तु आप �लखकर दे द�िजए। \n\n20 \nदे श म� भुखमर� क� िस्थ�त \n*2. श्री बलवंत उ फर् बाल आपटे: †† \n  श्री श्रीगोपाल व्यास : \nक्या कृ �ष मंत्री यह बताने क� कृ पा कर�गे �क : \n†† सभा म� यह प्रश्न श्री बलवंत उ फर् बाल आपटे द्वारा पूछा गया। \n(क) क्या यह सच है �क \"इंटरनेशनल फू ड पा�लसी �रसचर् इंस्ट�ट्यूट\" ने कहा है �क भारत म� 23 \nकरोड़ लोग भूख से पी�ड़त ह�; \n(ख) सरकार के पास इस �वषय से संबं�धत गत पांच वष� के राज्य-वार आंकड़े क्या ह�; \n(ग) क्या वतर्मान आ�थर्क प�रदृश्य को ध्यान म� रखते हुए इस िस्थ�त के और �बगड़ने क� संभावना \nहै; और \n(घ) य�द हां, तो इस समस्या से �नपटने क� योजना क्या है? \nकृ �ष मंत्री (श्री शरद पवार) : (क) से (घ) �ववरण सभा पटल पर रख �दया गया है। \n�ववरण \n(क) और (ख) नह�ं, श्रीमान जी। अन्तरार्ष्ट्र�य खाद्य नी�त अनुसंधान संस्थान \"भारत राज्य भूख \nसूचकांक राज्य म� भूख क� तुलना,\" पर अपने दस्तावेज (अक्तूबर 2008) म� उल्लेख करता है �क \nभारत म� 200 �म�लयन से अ�धक लोग ह� जो \"खाद्य असुर��त\" ह�। \nयोजना आयोग के अनुसार ऐसे लोग� क� संख्या के बारे म� कोई आ�धका�रक अनुमान नह�ं है जो \nदे श म� भुखमर� से ग्रस्त ह�। तथा�प, भारतीय प�रवार� म� खाद्य उपभोग क� अनुभव क� गई पयार्प्तता \n(1999-2000 हेतु �रपोटर् संख्या 466 तथा 2004-05 हेतु �रपोटर् संख्या 512) पर राष्ट्र�य प्र�तदशर् \nसव��ण संगठन क� �रपोटर् के अनुसार 1999-2000 से 2004-05 के दौरान ग्रामीण �ेत्र� म� ऐसे \nप�रवार� क� संख्या िजन्ह�ने पयार्प्त भोजन नह�ं �लया 3.3% से घटकर 2.4% हो गई और शहर� �ेत्र� म� \nऐसे प�रवार� क� संख्या 0.9% से घटकर 0.5% हो गई है। राज्यवार ब्यौरे ×�ववरण-I म� �दए गए ह� \n(नीचे दे �खए)। \n(ग) और (घ) चालू आ�थर्क प�रदृश्य से भारत पर शेष �वश्व क� तुलना म� बहुत कम असर पड़ने \nक� संभावना है क्य��क बहुत से सकारात्मक �नधर्न नी�त उपाय पहले से ह� कायार्न्वयन म� है। इस संबंध \nम� प्रमुख योजनाएं तथा कायर्क्रम ह� (i) राष्ट्र�य ग्रामीण रोजगार गारन्ट� अ�ध�नयम जो प्रत्येक ऐसे \nप�रवार िजसक े व्यस्क सदस्य अकु शल हस्त कायर् करने के इच्छु क ह�, के एक �वत्तीय वषर् म� कम से \nकम 100 �दन� क� मजदूर� रोजगार प्रदान करने क� गारन्ट� प्रदान करता है (ii) स्वरोजगार के सभी \nपहलुओं को शा�मल करते हुए स्वणर्जयन्ती ग्राम स्वरोजगार योजना (iii) छ: वषर् तक के बच्च�, \nगभर्वती म�हलाओं तथा स्तन्यदा माता को स्वास्थ्य, पोषण तथा शै��क सेवाओं का एक�कृ त प ै के ज प्रदान \nकरने के �लए एक�कृ त बाल �वकास सेवाएं (iv) बच्च� के बीच नामांकन, �नरंतरता तथा उपिस्थ�त \nबढ़ाने एवं साथ ह� उनक े पोषण स्तर� म� सुधार हेतु प्रारं भ क� गई मध्याह्न भोजन योजना (v) ल��त \nसावर्ज�नक �वतरण प्रणाल� समाज के गर�ब तथा कमजोर वग� को बाजार मूल्य� से बहुत कम मूल्य� पर \nखाद्यान्न प्रदान करता है। (v) अन्त्योदय अन्न योजना �नधर्न प�रवार� म� \n�नधर्नतम प�रवार� को बहुत \nअ�धक �रयायती दर पर खाद्यान्न प्रदान करता है (vi) राष्ट्र�य सामािजक सहायता कायर्क्रम का उद्देश्य \n\n21 \nगर�ब प�रवार� को वृद्धावस्था, कमाऊ व्यिक्त क� मृत्यु एवं प्रसू�त क� िस्थ�त म� सामािजक सहायता लाभ \nप्रदान करता है। सरकार ने �नधार्�रत मापदण्ड� के अनुसार गर�बी रे खा से नीचे के प�रवार� से संबं�धत \nव्यिक्तय� के कु छ वग� को क े न्द्र�य सहायता के रूप म� प्र�त लाभाथ� 200 रु. प्र�त माह क� दर से प�शन \nप्रदान करने के �लए राष्ट्र�य सामािजक सहायता कायर्क्रम के �ेत्र का �वस्तार करने का �नणर्य �लया है। \nदे श म� खाद्यान्न� का उत्पादन कु ल आबाद� क� मांग को पूरा करने के �लए पयार्प्त ह�। पुन: बढ़ती \nहुई मांग को पूरा करने के �लए दे श के अन्दर पयार्प्त उत्पादन सु�निश्चत करने हेतु, 2007-08 म� \nराष्ट्र�य खाद्य सुर�ा �मशन प्रारं भ �कया गया है िजसका उद्देश्य वषर् 2011-12 तक चावल, गेहूं तथा \nदाल� का उत्पादन क्रमश: 10 �म�लयन टन 8 �म�लयन टन एवं 2 �म�लयन टन तक बढ़ाना है।\n \n\n22 \n�ववरण-I \n1999-2000 तथा 2004-05 म� पयार्प्त खाद्य न उपलब्ध होने वाले प�रवार� क�  \nप्र�तशतता का राज्यवार ब्यौरा \nक्र.सं. राज्य पयार्प्त खाद्य न उपलब्ध होने वाले  पयार्प्त खाद्य न उपलब्ध होने  \n  प�रवार (1999-2000) (प्र�तशतता)वाले प�रवार (2004-2005)  (प्र�तशतता) \n  ग्रामीण शहर� ग्रामीण शहर� \n1. आंध्र प्रदे श 1.9 0.8 0.6 0.1 \n2. असम 7.1 1.5 5.3 2.4 \n3. �बहार 6.0 3.1 2.8 1.9 \n4. छत्तीसगढ़   2.6 0.1 \n5. गुजरात 0.5 0.9 0.3 0.0 \n6. ह�रयाणा 1.5 0.8 0.0 0.2 \n7. झारखण्ड   0.7 0.0 \n8. कनार्टक 0.9 0.2 0.3 0.1 \n9. के र ल 2.9 1.7 2.5 1.8 \n10. मध्य प्रदे श 3.1 1.2 2.0 0.4 \n11. महाराष्ट्र 1.9 0.6 1.0 0.5 \n12. उड़ीसा 8.0 2.5 6.1 0.6 \n13. पंजाब 0.2 0.1 0.9 0.2 \n14. राजस्थान 0.2 0.2 0.0 0.0 \n15. त�मलनाडु 0.7 0.3 0.2 0.4 \n16. उत्तर प्रदे श 2.1 1.0 1.7 0.8 \n17. पिश्चम बंगाल 10.4 2.0 11.9 1.3 \n अ�खल भारत 3.3 0.9 2.4 0.5 \nस्रोत : राष्ट्र�य प्र�तदशर् सव��ण �रपोटर् संख्या .466 भारतीय प�रवार� म� 1999-2000 म� खाद्य उपभाग क� अनुभव \nक� गई पयार्प्तता राष्ट्र�य  प्र�तदशर् सव��ण �रपोटर् संख्या. .512 भारतीय प�रवार� म� 2004-05 म� खाद्य उपभोग क� \nअनुभव क� गई पयार्प्तता \nStarvation in the country \n†*2. SHRI BALAVANT ALIAS BAL APTE:†† \n SHRI SHREEGOPAL VYAS: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the International Food Policy Research \nInstitute has stated that 23 crore people face starvation in India; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n23 \n††The question was actually asked on the floor of the House by Shri Balwant Alias \nBal Apte. \n\n24 \n(b) last five years' official figures thereon available with \nGovernment, State-wise details thereof; \n(c) whether this situation is likely to further deteriorate in view of \nthe current economic scenario; and \n(d) if so, the plan to tackle this problem? \nTHE MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI SHARAD PAWAR): (a) to (d) A \nStatement is laid on the Table of the House.  \nStatement \n(a) and (b) No, Sir. The International Food Policy Research Institute \n(IFPRI) in their paper (October, 2008) on \"The India State Hunger Index : \nComparison of Hunger Across States\", states there are more than 200 \nmillion people in India who are \"food insecure\". \nAs per Planning Commission, there are no official estimates of the \nnumber of people who face starvation in the country. However, as per the \nNSSO Reports on \"Perceived adequacy of food consumption in Indian \nHouseholds (Report No.466 for 1999- 2000 and Report No.512 for 2004- 05), \nthe percentage of households in rural areas who did not get enough food  \nhas declined from 3.3% to 2.4% and in the urban areas, the percentage of \nsuch households has declined from 0.9% to 0.5% during 1999- 2000 to 2004-\n05. State-wise details are given in Statement-I (See below). \n(c) and (d) The current economic scenario is expected to impact India \nmuch less severely than rest of the world since several pro- poor policy \nmeasures are already under implementation. The major schemes and \nprogrammes in this regard are: (i) National Rural Employment Guarantee \nAct (NREGA), which provides a guarantee of at least 100 days of wage \nemployment in a financial year to every household whose adult members \nvolunteer to do unskilled manual work, (ii) Swarnjayanti Gram Swarozgar \nYojana covering all aspects of self- employment, (iii) Integrated Child \nDevelopment Services to provide an integrated package of health, \nnutrition and educational services to children up to six years of age, \npregnant women and nursing mothers, (iv) Mid- day Meal Scheme, introduced \nto enhance enrolment, retention and attendance and simultaneously \nimproving nutritional levels among the children, (v) The Targeted Public \nDistribution System (TPDS) provides food grains at much lower than the \nmarket prices to the poor and vulnerable sections of the society, (vi) \nThe Antyodaya Anna Yojana (AAY) provides food grains to the poorest of \nthe poor families at a highly subsidized rate, (vii) The National Social \nAssistance Programme (NSAP) aims at providing social assistance benefits \n\n25 \nto poor households in the case of old age, death of the breadwinner and \nmaternity. The Government has decided to expand the scope of NSAP to \nprovide pension at the rate of Rs. 200 per month as Central assistance \nper beneficiary to certain categories of persons belonging to household \nbelow the poverty line according to the criteria prescribed. \nThe production of foodgrains in the country is adequate to meet the \ndemand of the total population. Further, to ensure adequate production \nwithin the country to meet the increasing demand, the 'National Food \nSecurity Mission' was launched in 2007- 08 which aims at increasing \nproduction of rice, wheat and pulses by 10 million tonnes, 8 million \ntonnes and 2 million tonnes respectively by 2011-12. \n\n26 \nStatement-I \nStatewise details of Percentage of Households Not getting  \nenough Food in 1999-2000 & 2004-05 \nSl.   States Households Not getting Households Not getting  \nNo.  enough Food (1999-2000)  enough Food (2004-05) \n  (Percentage)  (Percentage) \n  Rural Urban Rural Urban \n1. Andhra Pradesh 1.9 0.8 0.6 0.1 \n2. Assam  7.1 1.5 5.3 2.4 \n3. Bihar  6.0 3.1 2.8 1.9 \n4. Chhattisgarh   2.6 0.1 \n5. Gujarat 0.5 0.9 0.3 0.0 \n6. Haryana 1.5 0.8 0.0 0.2 \n7. Jharkhand   0.7 0.0 \n8. Karnataka 0.9 0.2 0.3 0.1 \n9. Kerala 2.9 1.7 2.5 1.8 \n10. Madhya Pradesh 3.1 1.2 2.0 0.4 \n11. Maharashtra 1.9 0.6 1.0 0.5 \n12. Orissa 8.0 2.5 6.1 0.6 \n13. Punjab 0.2 0.1 0.9 0.2 \n14. Rajasthan 0.2 0.2 0.0 0.0 \n 15. Tamil Nadu 0.7 0.3 0.2 0.4 \n16. Uttar Pradesh 2.1 1.0 1.7 0.8 \n17. West Bengal 10.4 2.0 11.9 1.3 \nAll India 3.3 0.9 2.4 0.5 \nSOURCE: NSS Report No. 466; Perceived Adequacy of Food Consumption in Indian \nHouseholds  \n          1999-2000. \nNSS Report No. 512; Perceived Adequacy of Food Consumption in Indian Households \n2004-05. \nSHRI BALAVANT alias BAL APTE: Sir, to deal with the question of \nstarvation, it needs a comprehensive policy framework, along with food, \nagriculture and farmers. Today, we find that all these aspects are \nconsidered in a disjointed way. Therefore, the process of planning gets \naffected. On the one hand, you tell the farmers that they need not depend \n\n27 \non agriculture. You want the population, depending on agriculture, to be \nreduced; on the other hand, the farmer is given, if at all, only a \nminimum price that will help him survive. There is no profit in it. He \ncan't produce more. Therefore,  is  there  any policy framework in the \nmind of the Government that will deal with starvation  \n\n28 \ndirectly, that will give the farmers profit- making remunerations, which \nwill give the agriculture the necessary priority, and where food will be \na matter of concern? Is there any such framework in the mind of the \nGovernment? Is the Minister in a position to spell it out? \nSHRI SHARAD PAWAR: Sir, there are three aspects about the farmers and \nfarmers' position, how to improve the productivity, and, ultimately, how \nto resolve the problem of the food security of this country. The most \nimportant thing is the availability of the food and production of the \nfood. This Government has taken a conscious decision to provide a better \nprice to the farmers. One can just see the prices that were provided for \nwheat last year. It was somewhat Rs. 1000/-  per quintal. It was, in the \nnext year, raised by Rs. 300/-  per quintal. Similar is the situation in \nthe case of rice, oilseeds, pulses, sugarcane, and practically each and \nevery crop. Because of this farmer- friendly policy, one can see that in \nthe year 2005- 06, the total production in this country was 208 million \ntonnes; in the year 2006 -07, it had gone to 217 million tonnes; and, in \nthe year 2007- 08, it had gone to 230 million tonnes. So, there was 6.2 \nper cent growth in the year 2007-08. This is a clear-cut indication as to \nwhy there was more production. It was mainly because of the proper prices \nprovided to the farmers. \nNow, I come to the issue of starvation. The part 'a' of the question \nsays, \"Whether it is a fact that the International Food Policy Research \nInstitute has stated that 23 crore people face starvation in India;\". In \nfact, I tried to collect the International Food Policy Research \nInstitute's report. They had published a paper in October 2008. Its \nsubject is, \"The India State Hunger Index– Comparison of Hunger Across \nStates.\" The actual statement which they have made does not say that it \nis 23 crores, and, secondly, they have not mentioned that as \n'starvation'. Their statement states that there are more than 200 million \npeople in India who are 'food insecure'. They have mentioned it as 'food \ninsecure', and there is a difference between the 'starvation' and 'the \nfood insecure'. The availability of foodgrains is there. I have to accept \none thing that there are sizable sections in the society where the \navailability of food is there but they have a limited purchasing power \nand, therefore, they are not in a position to afford the purchase as per \ntheir requirement. So, I am not in a position to deny that factual \nposition. That is a factual position, and, in fact, in the last year or \n\n29 \nin the year before the last year, globally, the foodgrain prices had gone \nup like anything and that has affected the poor sections of the society, \nnot only in India but also throughout the globe. The FAO had called a \nseparate meeting for that purpose and taken certain actions. \nBut forget about what is happening outside India. In our country with \nregard to the section which is facing the problem of food insecurity, the \nGovernment of India has taken many decisions and this is not only today. \nThe previous Government had also taken many decisions; today's Government \nhas improved upon these decisions and one can see it. If we take the case \nof IFPRI, they have said that a population of 23 crore comes under the \ncategory of 'food insecurity'. Sir, let us anal yse this figure of '23 \ncrore'. The figure of '23 crore' is not accepted by the Planning \nCommission and this figure is also not  accepted  by  the  NSSO.  But  \nstill I am ready to accept this for calculation purposes. They have  \n\n30 \nsaid that a population of 23 crore is affected. Now, to help those \npeople, under the AAY, Antyodaya Anna Yojana, we are providing foodgrains \n- 35 kg per family; wheat at two rupees per kilo and rice at three rupees \nper kilo -  to 13 crore people. Under the BPL, where we are providing \nfoodgrains at cheaper rate, we have covered a population of 21 crore. So, \nthis figure combined together -  the number of people covered under AAY, \nBPL plus the number of people covered under the Old Age Pension, which is \naround 1.57 crore - comes to around 36 crore. The IFPRI is saying that a \npopulation of 23 crore is affected. So, we have already taken care of 36 \ncrore people and then there are some other schemes where some alternative \narrangements have been made which has been given in detail here \n...(Interruptions)... \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Second supplementary, please. ...(Interruptions)... \nPlease, please, we are coming to it. \nSHRI BALAVANT ALIAS BAL APTE: Sir, this answer itself shows that on \nmany occasions, statistics do fudge the realities plus we are talking \nabout 200 million and 23 crores; it is all the same. One is mentioned in \nmillions and the other is mentioned in crores. That's all. But the fact \nremains that that figure is given and 'food insecure' is a sophisticated \nway to refer to 'starvation' like these days, to respect the individual, \nwe don't call him 'blind', we call him 'visually challenged', but the \nreality is starvation. So, let us not fudge over it and if it is 23 \ncores, then yes, it is 23 crores. But the answer says that there are no \nofficial estimates in the Planning Commission. \nNow, in a poor country, where poverty and starvation go together, we \ndon't have any official estimates. So, we are not addressing the problem \nat all. Sir, there are statistics and statistics. The same agency has \nsaid that the agricultural GDP may decrease by 16 per cent within the \nnext ten years. The same says that at the global hunger index, India is \n66. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: What is the supplementary question? \nSHRI BALAVANT ALIAS BAL APTE: Sir, the su pplementary question, \ntherefore, is whether the Government finds it necessary to know what the \nchallenge before the people is by an official statistical estimate of \npoverty as well as hunger and whether the Government will ensure that \n\n31 \nsuch official statistical estimate is available by actually going to the \npeople, not sitting in the offices, as sometimes happen with many of the \nGovernment statistics which are made in the offices. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Please put the question. You have asked the \nsupplementary question. Let it be answered. \nSHRI BALAVANT ALIAS BAL APTE: Sir, the specific question is whether \nGovernment has any plans to have statistics on this, so that they could \nmeet the challenges. They don't know the challenges at all. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: All right. That is the specific question. \n\n32 \nSHRI SHARAD PAWAR: It is a good thing that they are now accepting \nstatistics. Earlier there were remarks that these are statistics alone. \nAs stated in the reply, the Planning Commission does not have any \nofficial estimates of the number of people who face starvation. However, \nour NSS Organisations do collect information every year about population \nliving below the Poverty Line, that is, BPL. So, Government of India, as \nalso the State Governments, regularly get that report and figures, and \nthat is the reason why various types of schemes have been introduced. I \nhave already given information regarding availability of foodgrains at \ncheaper rates; it is the responsibility of the State Governments as also \nthe Government of India to provide them, particularly to the vulnerable \nsections of society. Now, who constitute the vulnerable sections of the \nsociety? The AAY scheme is meant for that category. I have already stated \nin the House that to that particular category we are providing 35 kg of  \nfoodgrains at the rate of two or three rupees per kilo, especially wheat \nand rice. So, that is definitely a support to this section. And we \nprovide the same quantity at different rates as well to BPL families. So, \nGovernment has taken enough measures for these sections and made \nallocations to all the States; there is no reduction in allocation for \nthese two categories. After getting sufficient food stock from the \nGovernment of India, their distribution is the responsibility of the \nState Governments. Distribution at the village level or at the lowest \nlevel is the job of the State Governments. Our job is to supply \nfoodgrains at a reasonable rate, which we are doing. \nSHRI BALAVANT ALIAS BAL APTE: Sir, these days poverty is being \nmarketed by films and books. Let Government give a clear picture about \npoverty. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: You have asked your question. Please allow your \ncolleague to ask the second supplementary. Shri Shreegopal Vyas. \nश्री श्रीगोपाल व्यास: धन्यवाद सभाप�त महोदय, म� माननीय मंत्री जी से यह जानना चाहता हूं �क \nग्याहरवीं पंचवष�य योजना म� योजना आयोग के साथ बैठकर यह �वचार-�वमशर् हुआ है �क प्रत्येक िजले \nपर, िजले क� िस्थ�त के अनुसार योजना बनाई जाएगी और क� द्र भी उसम� अ�धक योगदान करे गा। अभी \nस्वास्थ्य मंत्रालय ने यू�नसेफ के ज�रए एक सव�\n कराया है �क इस दे श म� तीन साल से छोटे 46% बच्चे \nकु पोषण के �शकार ह�। म� माननीय मंत्री जी से यह पूछना चाहता हूं और दे श के �लए आश्वासन भी \nचाहता हूं �क यह जो िजलेवार योजना ग्यारहवीं पंचवष�य योजना म� बनाने जा रहे ह�, यह अच्छ� बात \nहै, परं तु क्या इसम� क� द्र का योगदान इतना अवश्य होगा �क ये बच्चे कु पोषण के �शकार न ह�, कृ पया \nयह आश्वासन हम� दे द�िजए। \nश्री शरद पवार: सभाप�त जी, एग्रीकल्चर प्रोडक्शन बढ़ाने के �लए भारत सरकार ने दे श के सभी \n\n33 \nचीफ �म�नस्टसर् क� एक मी�टंग बुलाकर �ड�सजन ले �लया है on 19th May, the year before \nlast year, where Rs. 25,000 crores have been provided for enhancing food \nproduction in the country. One of the decisions taken there was that \nState Government which would like to submit their proposals to Government \nof India need to prepare District Plans and the amount equivalent to the \ncontribution that a State Government proposes to make would be provided \nby Government of India. Implementation of this particular scheme has been \nstarted since last year and our first year's experience has been quite \ngood.  Most  of  the  States  are  taking  advantage  of this. We \nobserved that there is improvement in  \n\n34 \nfood production and that is the reason why we had reached the mark of 213 \nmillion tonnes last year; highest procurement was made in the last year. \nSo, the first job is definitely done by the respective State Governments \nwith the support of the Government of India. Now, coming to the question \nof children, I will not be able to give detailed information about this, \nbut there are certain schemes which have been introduced in this country \nlike the Mid-day Meal Scheme in ICSE, and it is the responsibility of the \nFood Ministry to provide foodgrains to them. We are supplying the \nfoodgrains to all the schemes on their demand and the various other \ndepartments and institutions are executing the schemes.  \nMS. MABEL REBELLO: I was listening to the hon. Minister. He has been \nsaying that the foodgrains production has increased. Sir, the Government \nof India has given a commitment to the UN. As per the UN Millennium \nDevelopment Goal, India has to decline the percentage of population \nsuffering from hunger by 2015. Today we are in 2009 and absolute number \nof people suffering from hunger is increasing instead of declining. Will \nyou be able to achieve the UN Millennium Development Goal by 2015? I \nwould like to know that. \nSHRI SHARAD PAWAR: I can definitely say that it is planned to enhance \nfood production in this country. Of course, one thing I have to make \nabsolutely clear that sometimes foodgrains production declines because of \nthe seasonal factors like uncertainty about the monsoon and so on. We are \ntaking all the precautions that we are supposed to take from the \nGovernment of India like availability of credit, availability of seed, \navailability of fertilizers, proper prices for the farmer and all sorts \nof incentives to the farmers. From all these angles, all precautions have \nbeen taken and we are getting results, and I am confident that \n...(Interruptions)... \nMS. MABEL REBELLO: But our number should decline. Instead this number \nis increasing. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI SHARAD PAWAR: You are asking about the production. So, I am \nconfident that whatever the commitment has been made to the UN we will \nreach to that stage by 2015. That planning has been done and the \nimplementation has been started and we started getting results. Now so \n\n35 \nfar as the question of starvation or hunger or the availability of food \nis concerned, definitely allocation will be made more and our efforts \nwill be there to achieve this particular percentage. The latest report of \nthe NSSO shows that that percentage is going down. \nMS. MABEL REBELLO: No, Sir ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRIMATI BRINDA KARAT: Sir, my question relates to part 5 of the \nMinister's answer in which he said that the targeted Public Distribution \nSystem provides foodgrains at a price which is much lower than the market \nprice to the  poorand vulnerable  sections  of  the  society, सर, मेरा सवाल \nयह है  \n\n36 \n�क \"poor and vulnerable sections\" क� प�रभाषा क्या है? मंत्री जी ने अपने जवाब म� यह \nकहा, \"I am not in a position to state that people have the purchasing \npower to buy available foodgrains.\" आपने स्वीकार �कया �क लोग� क� जेब म� पैसा है या \nनह�ं है, इ स के बारे म� आप कोई आश्वासन नह�ं दे सकते ह�। इसी सदन म� अजुर्न सेनगुप्त कमीशन क� \n�रपोटर् पर बहस करते वक्त यह आंकड़ा सामने आया है �क आज 77 प्र�तशत लोग� के पास \npurchasing power का जो मापदण्ड है, वह यह है �क वे एक �दन म� 20 रुपए से कम खचर् करते \nह�। मंत्री जी से मेरा कहना यह है �क आपने 36 करोड़ को cover �कया है। APL section क� \nप�रभाषा यह है �क जो लोग ग्रामीण �ेत्र म� 11 रुपए से ज्यादा कमाते ह�, वे APL म� आते ह�। वे \nआपक� सरकार� प�रभाषा म� poor and vulnerable sections म � नह�ं आते ह�। जब आप स्वयं \nयह स्वीकार कर रहे ह� �क लोग� क� जेब म� purchasing power नह�ं है, तो क्या आप कम-से-कम \nअपने BPL अंत्योदय का आज जो मापदण्ड है, उसको बढ़ा कर कम-से-कम उस 77 प्र�तशत जनता \nतक पहुंचाने का कदम उठाएंगे या नह�ं? कृ पया आप इसका जवाब द�िजए। \nश्री शरद पवार:  सर, जैसा म�ने शुरू म� कहा �क it is true that there are certain \nsections in the society whose purchasing power is weak and that is the \nreason why special precaution has been taken. We are not neglecting that. \nNow the question comes of those who come above the \"poverty line. It is \ntrue that whatever allocation has been made to the States for AAY- BPL is \none allocation and that is 35 kg. and the allocation for the APL depends \non the availability. In some States it is 9 kg. or 10 kg. and some States \nare not accepting and that is why it is lower also. For instance, take \nthe case of rice. Sir, there is no shortage of wheat. As on today, \nwhatever demand is coming from any State in this country for allocation \nof additional quantity of wheat, we are accepting that and we are \nproviding that. That is not the issue. But, till last year, there was a \nserious problem about the availability of rice. There are certain States, \nparticularly, in the southern India, where there is more demand for rice, \nand, serious problem was being faced. We are improving our procurement \nthis year, we started enhancing the quota and giving additional \nallocation. I will be happy to collect all this information as to how \nmuch additional rice, in the last six months, has been given to Kerala, \nTamil Nadu. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRIMATI BRINDA KARAT: Will you extend the BPL category status to \nseventy seven per cent of the population? That is my question, Sir. I am \nnot asking about the APL. You said, the purchasing power. \n...(Interruptions)... I am simply asking that, Sir. \nSHRI SHARAD PAWAR: No, no. It is the conscious decision of the \nGovernment of India to, at least, target the...(Interruptions)... We have \ntargeted the vulnerable sections. ...(Interruptions)... \n\n37 \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Please don't interrupt. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI SHARAD PAWAR: No, it is not. The targeted public distribution \nsystem. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRIMATI BRINDA KARAT: Sir, seventy- seven per cent of the population \non less than 20 rupees a day! What is poor and vulnerable? \nSHRI SHARAD PAWAR: Sir, when the policy was changed about 7- 8 years \nago, a conscious decision  was  taken  by the Government to introduce the \ntargeted public distribution system. What is  \n\n38 \ntargeted? Where is the target? It is targeted towards the weaker \nsections. So, we are taking care of the weaker sections. In fact, this \nyear, our food subsidy Bill will go practically to somewhere near Rs. 49 \nto 50 thousand crores from Rs. 19,000 crores. So, we are providing that \ntype of subsidy and the reason is that we would like to protect the \ninterests of the targeted sections first, and, if there is more \nproduction, certainly, we will look into it and try to protect the \ninterests of other sections. ...(Interruptions)... \nSHRIMATI BRINDA KARAT: Sir, your buffer stock is eighty per cent more \nnow. Surplus stock is there. \nMR. CHAIRMAN: Shri N.K. Singh. \nSHRI N.K. SINGH: Sir, I wish to draw the attention of the hon. \nMinister to the statement contained in Statement- I of his reply, in \nwhich, he will notice that the data stops at the year 2004- 05. Sir, the \ndata is missing for the last four important years since it is shown up to \n2004-05 only. Is the Minister aware that international reports, \nparticularly, from the FAO and the United Nations, suggest that on \naccount of unabated inflation in the last two years and the global \nmeltdown, 200 million people have gone back into starvation and have gone \nback below the poverty syndrome? Is the Minister, therefore, aware of the \nneed to have more contemporary data to assess the actual incidence of the \nincreased burden on account of the economic events during the last two \nyears? \nSHRI SHARAD PAWAR: Sir, I am fully aware about one thing. Year before \nlast year, FAO had published a data, which mentioned that because of the \nhigh-rising prices globally, a sizable section –  I do not recollect \nexactly but, I think, it is 75 million population has been affected. It \nis the addition to the population which is facing serious problem of food \ninsecurity, and, the reason, essentially, was the prices of the \nfoodgrains. \nIn fact, we have adopted a two- pronged strategy. We want to improve \nour production. There is no choice; we have to give incentive to the \nfarmer, we have to give better prices. We have provided better prices. \nSimultaneously, we have provided substantial subsidy for the public \ndistribution system, especially, to that section which is not in a \nposition to buy it at a particular rate. That is why, at cheaper rates, \n\n39 \nfoodgrains have been supplied to all the States, which come under these \ntwo categories, about which I have already given you the information. We \nare fully aware about that report. We are fully aware about the issue. \nBut, now the trend, which we are observing in our own country, is that \nthe prices are going down. This is what we have seen in the inflation \nrate. ...(Interruptions)... Yes, including food also. If there is. \n...(Interruptions)... \nSHRI PRAKASH JAVADEKAR: No, Sir. The prices of food items are not \ngoing down. \nSHRI SHARAD PAWAR: I can tell you, in the last two years, or, one \nyear, there has been no change in the wheat prices throughout India. You \ncan check this. There is a definite. ...(Interruptions)... I do not say \nthat. ...(Interruptions)... \n\n40 \nAvailability of fertilizers in the country \n*3. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Will the Minister of CHEMICALS AND \nFERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government has curbed imports of fertilizers to keep \nfertilizers subsidy, within reasonable limits as the international prices \nof fertilizers have gone up by a factor of 2 to 3 times, resulting in a \nsteep increase in the quantum of the fertilizer subsidy; \n(b) if so, whether this has resulted in a shortage of fertilizers in \nthe country and led to violent agitation by farmers; \n(c) whether fertilizers is being distributed in some places from \npolice stations; and \n(d) what steps is Government taking to ease farmers' problems in this \nregard? \nTHE MINISTER OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS (SHRI RAM VILAS PASWAN): (a) \nto (d) A Statement is laid on the Table of the House. \nStatement \n(a) No, Sir. The Details regarding import of fertilizers during the \nyears 2006-07 to 2008-09 are at Statement-I (See below). \n(b) Does not arise. The requirement, availability and sales of major \nfertilizers in the country for the year 2008- 09 (April, 2008 to \nJanuary, 2009) are at Statement-II (see below). \n(c) Urea is the only fertilizer which is under partial movement and \ndistribution control of the Government. Union Government ensures \navailability of urea at State level. State Governments are responsible \nand take appropriate measures for its distribution within the State. \n(d) The steps taken by the Department of Fertilizers for smooth \ndistribution of fertilizers are as under: \n i) Movement of fertilizers is being monitored throughout the country \nby an on-line web based monitoring system to track production, \nimports, dispatch, arrival and sales up to the district level. The \ndata is also accessible to public at www.urvarak.co.in \n ii) The subsidy on fertilizers is to be paid only when it reaches the \ndistrict; \n iii) Department of Fertilizers operates Buffer Stock of urea in major \nurea consuming states, up to a limit of 5% of the seasonal \n\n41 \nrequirement through State Institutional Agencies / fertilizer \ncompanies; \n iv) The gap between requirement and indigenous availability of Urea, \nDAP and MOP is met through imports; \n v) Department of Fertilizers contacts the authorities of the State \nAgriculture Department everyday to find out any shortage of \nfertilizers anywhere in the state and based on the feed back \nreceived immediate remedial steps are taken; \n\n42 \n vi) Periodic Review meetings on availability of fertilizers are held \nbetween the Officers of the State Agriculture Department and \nfertilizer suppliers by Secretary (Fertilizer) to resolve the \nproblems, if any, faced by the S tate Government / fertilizer \nsuppliers. \n vii) The Department of Fertilizers has been facilitating the State \nGovernments in procurement of the decontrolled fertilizers through \nindigenous manufacturers and importers. \n viii) Policy for uniform freight subsidy on all fertilizers \nunder the fertilizer subsidy regime has been implemented from 1st \nApril, 2008. \nStatement-I \nDetails regarding import of fertilizers during the year 2006-2009 \n(Figures in Lakh Metric Tonnes) \n  Year-wise import of Urea, DAP & MOP \nYear Urea DAP MOP \n2006-07 47.18 28.41 34.48 \n2007-08 69.26 29.78 44.31 \n2008-09* 57.13 64.92 44.00 \n* estimated \nStatement-II \nDetails of requirement, availability and sales of major fertilizers in \nthe  \ncountry for the year 2008-09 (April, 2008 to January, 2009) \n(figures in Lakh Metric Tonnes) \nAll India Product Requirement  Availability  Sales to State  \n   Indigenous Imports Total agency/ dealers /  \n      stockiest \n Urea 253.99 177.79 54.08 231.87 226.27 \n DAP 88.23 32.29 60.05 92.34 91.98 \n MOP 33.00  37.16 37.16 35.06 \n Complex 84.11 65.70  65.70 64.47 \nSHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Sir, did this curb on import affect the \navailability of fertilizer to farmers on the one hand and hoarding and \nundue profiteering by dealers on the other hand forcing farmers to resort \nto an agitation in Kharif season? What steps are being taken to increase \nthe production of fertilizer in the country? \nश्री राम �वलास पासवान: सर, इन्ह�ने प्रश्न (क) म� पूछा था �क आयात म� कमी हुई है या नह�ं, \n\n43 \nतो हमने बताया �क फ�टर्लाइजर के आयात म� कमी नह�ं हुई है। इ स के �वपर�त आप य�द दे ख�गे �क \nपहले हम यू�रया का जो आयात करते थे, जो 2006-2007 म� 47 लाख टन था, वह अभी तक 57 \nलाख टन हुआ है तथा यह और भी होगा, डी.ए.पी. का आयात हमने �पछले वषर् 29 लाख टन �कया \nथा और इस वषर् 64 लाख टन आयात �कया है तथा एम.ओ.पी.  \n\n44 \nका आयात 44 लाख टन �कया था, जो हमने अभी तक 44 लाख टन आयात �कया है। यह बात सह� \nहै �क जो राज्य सहायता है, उसम� हम जहां �पछले साल 36 हजार करोड़ रुपए क� टोटल सिब्सडी दे \nरहे थे, इस साल �कसान� के �