details thereof; \n(c) whether the view of experts/NGOs in identifying the exact cause of \nsuicide by farmers have been taken note of; \n(d) if so, whether any new study would be conducted to ascertain the \nexact cause of spate of suicide by farmers in the country; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) to (e) The suicides by farmers have been reported from the \nStates of Andhra Pradesh, Maharashtra, Karnataka and Gujarat only in \n2008-09. The State Governments have broadly reported crop failure, \nindebtedness, drought, social and economic insecurities as causes for \nsuicides by farmers, as also identified in various study reports in this \ncontext e.g. Tata Institute of Social Sciences (TISS), Indira Gandhi \nInstitute of Development Research (IGIDR), Mumbai, National Institutes \nfor Rural Development (NIRD), National Institute of Agricultural \nExtension & Management (MANAGE), Hyderabad and Veeresh Committee etc. \nFurther, the Veeresh Committee in context of farmers' suicides in \nKarnataka has stated that connecting the incidents of suicides directly \nto any single cause would be erroneous, since it is a personal decision \nof the victim and the post suicide search for causes gives only \ncircumstantial evidence. \nRecord production of wheat and rice \n13. DR. T. SUBBARAMI REDDY: \n SHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether there has been a record wheat and paddy production in \nPunjab and other wheat growing States and rice procurement has crossed \n143 lakh tonne which is up by 21 per cent; \n(b) if so, whether wheat sowing has been completed at 27 million \nhectare; \n(c) if so, whether wheat procurement has reached an all time record of \n\n131 \nover 25 million tonne; \n(d) if so, whether in view of this increased production of wheat and \nrice, Government is considering to ease the curbs on export of wheat and \nrice; and \n(e) if so, the latest position and whether country has become self-\nsufficient in wheat and rice? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY  OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) As per 4th advance estimates for 2007- 08 released on 9th \nJuly, 2008, production of wheat  \nand  rice at All India level are estimated to be at record levels of \n78.40 million tonnes and 96.43 million  \n\n132 \nonnes. As per 1st Advance Estimates for 2008- 09 released on 25th \nSeptember, 2008, production of rice during Kharif season of 2008- 09 has \nbeen estimated at 83.25 million tonnes. State- wise details of production \nof wheat and rice during 2007- 08 (Kharif + Rabi) and 2008- 09 (Kharif \nonly) are given in the Statement (See below). \nThere has been a record procurement of 28.49 million tonnes of rice \nduring Kharif Marketing Season 2007- 08. In the current Kharif Marketing \nSeason 2008-09, 21.46 million tonnes of rice has been procured up to \n9.2.2009 against 18.17 million tonnes of rice procured during the \ncorresponding period of the Kharif Marketing Season 2007-08. \n(b) As per the assessment reported to the Crop Weather Watch Group \n(CWWG), the area sown under wheat during Rabi 2008- 09 is estimated at \n27.6 million hectares. \n(c) There has been a record procurement of 22.68 million tonnes of \nwheat during Rabi Marketing Season 2008-09. \n(d) and (e) Keeping in view the increased production and procurement \nof wheat and rice, the Government has decided to allow export of upto \n2.00 million tonnes of wheat from Central Pool on diplomatic \nconsiderations. The export duty on basmati rice has been removed and \nMinimum Export Price of basmati rice has been reduced from $ 1200 per \ntonne to $ 1100 per tonne. Currently, the country is self sufficient in \nwheat and rice. \nStatement \nState-wise Estimates of production of Rice and Wheat during  \n2007-2008 and 2008-2009 \n('000 Tonnes) \nState                                   Rice  Wheat\n 2007-08* 2008-09$ 2007-08* \n  (Kharif only) \n1 2 3 4 \nAndhra Pradesh 13226.0 7627.0 7.0 \nAssam 2783.0 2563.0 63.0 \nBihar 4495.0 3919.0 4414.0 \nChhattisgarh 5426.0 5278.0 99.0 \nGujarat 1491.0 1372.0 3764.0 \nHaryana 3613.0 3958.0 10293.0 \nHimachal Pradesh 121.0 116.0 608.0 \n\n133 \nJammu & Kashmir 564.0 553.0 474.0 \nJharkhand 3015.0 3095.0 130.0 \nKarnataka 3548.0 2278.0 241.0 \nKerala 546.0 498.0 N G \n\n134 \n1 2 3 4 \nMadhya Pradesh 1285.0 1336.0 6267.0 \nMaharashtra 2967.0 2830.0 2081.0 \nOrissa 7507.0 6079.0 11.0 \nPunjab 10489.0 10496.0 15744.0 \nRajasthan 260.0 178.0 7125.0 \nTamil Nadu 6227.0 5693.0 NG \nUttar Pradesh 11741.0 12448.0 25432.0 \nUttarakhand 573.0 578.0 707.0 \nWest Bengal 14668.0 10608.0 820.0 \nOthers 1887.0 1750.0 121.0 \nAll-India 96431.0 83254.0 78401.0 \n* 4th Advance Estimates released on 09.07.2008 \n$ 1st Advance Estimates released on 25.09.2008 \nNG: Not Grown \nMeasures to prevent bird-flu \n†14. SHRI MOTI LAL VORA: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) the steps taken by G overnment in view of frequent threats of \nspread of bird-flu; \n(b) whether it is also a fact that bird- flu had spread to some parts \nof the country in November-December, 2008 also; \n(c) the estimated loss to States affected therefrom; \n(d) whether some countries have stopped import of poultry products \nfrom India in view of bird-flu; and \n(e) whether Government has taken steps to maintain co- ordination with \nthe neighboring countries, so that the bird- flu could be effectively \nchecked? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI \nTASLIMUDDIN): (a) The steps taken by the Government in this regard, inter \nalia, include surveillance of poultry against the disease, training of \nveterinary personnel, upgradation of existing laboratories and \nestablishment of new ones and spreading awareness in the general public \non precautions required in handling poultry. \n(b) Yes, Sir. \n(c) The loss on account of the outbreak of bird- flu includes, poultry \nlost due to culling, loss of income of poultry farmers, input suppliers \nand traders, loss due to decline in prices of poultry and poultry \n\n135 \nproducts in the affected areas and in the rest of the country loss, of \nexport income, loss due to aborted growth of the poultry industry and \nloss due to decline in investor confidence in the industry. \n(d) and (e) Yes, Sir. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n136 \nNew food policy \n†15. SHRI JAI PARKASH AGGARWAL: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Government proposes to formulate a comprehensive food \npolicy in view of instability in the production of foodgrains, pulses and \nsugar; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) by when the new food policy is likely to be formulated? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) to (c) There is no proposal under consideration to formulate \na new food policy. The production of foodgrains, pulses and sugar during \nthe last three financial years indicates a steady increase and as per the \nFourth Advance Estimates for the year 2007- 08, the production of \nfoodgrains and pulses is at an all- time record. However, the Government \nalready has a comprehensive food policy under which Minimum Support Price \n(MSP) operations are carried out by the Government agencies in districts \nall over the country where there is marketable surplus of foodgrains. \nFarmers have the option to sell their produce at MSP or in the open \nmarket, as is advantageous to them. This policy ensures that farmers get \nremunerative prices for their produce. This also enables the Government \nto procure foodgrains for servicing the Targeted Public Distribution \nSystem and welfare schemes. Maintenance of a buffer stock of foodgrains \nto ensure food security is also one of the important objectives of the \nfood policy. Further, the Government has launched the 'National Food \nSecurity Mission' with the aim of increasing production of wheat, rice \nand pulses through area expansion and productivity enhancement; restoring \nsoil fertility and productivity; creating employment opportunities and \nenhancing farm- level economy to restore confidence of farmers. The \nMission is presently being implemented in 312 identified districts of 17 \nStates of the country. \nFarming technique for small holding farmers \n†16. SHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \n DR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that 80 per cent of agricultural land in the \ncountry is divided into parts of 1.5 hectares each; \n(b) if not, the actual fact in this regard; \n(c) whether the production technique based on machines can be \nbeneficial for the agricultural production in farms of small holdings; \n\n137 \nand \n(d) if so, Government's response thereto and the technique being \nsuggested by scientists to farmers having small holdings for increase in \nproduction? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) and (b) According to the 7th quinquennial Agriculture Census \n2000-01, operational holdings in the country in the category of 'below \n2.0 hectares' were 81.8 per cent of the total operational holdings. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n138 \n(c) Yes, Sir. \n(d) Keeping in view the small farm holdings, a good number of manual, \nanimal drawn and power tiller operated farm equipment for land \npreparation, sowing, spraying, harvesting and threshing have been \ndeveloped in the country. The Indian Council of Agricultural Research \n(ICAR) is operating two All India Coordinated Research Projects (AICRPs), \nnamely, AICRP on Utilization of Animal Energy (UAE) having 7 centres and \nAICRP on Farm Implements and Machinery (FIM) having 23 centres located in \ndifferent State Agricultural Universities and ICAR Institutes to develop \nlocation specific farm tools and equipment to meet the needs of the \nsmall, medium and large farmers in the country. \nEncouragement for organic farming \n†17. SHRI BHAGAT SINGH KOSHYARI: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether any scheme to announce minimum support price of various \ncereals, pulses, oilseeds, vegetables and fruits to encourage organic \nfarming, is under Government's consideration, so that the farmers could \nget fair price for their produce; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) and (b) There is no scheme under Government's consideration \nto announce minimum support price of organically produced cereals, \npulses, oilseeds, vegetables and fruits. \n(c) The Government is already promoting organic farming and the growth \nin demand for organic produce is substantial. \nImpurity of milk \n†18. SHRI MOTILAL VORA: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that according to the scientists of National \nDairy Research Institute (NDRI) milk produced by 30 to 37 per cent \ncattles is impure and causes spread of diseases; \n(b) whether according to the scientists of NDRI the reason behind it \nis lack of awareness amongst cattlemen; \n(c) whether Government is planning to create awareness among \n\n139 \ncattlemen; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) to (d) No, Sir. NDRI scientists was misquoted in a newspaper \nagainst which the Institute issued necessary clarification. However, to \ncreate awareness amongst cattlemen, NDRI regularly conducts training  for  \ndairy  farmers  on  different  facets  of  clean  milk  production. The \nDepartment of Animal  \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n140 \n Husbandry, Dairying & Fisheries through the centrally sponsored scheme \n\"Strengthening infrastructure for quality and clean milk production\" is \nalso training and strengthening the infrastructure to create mass \nawareness about importance of clean milk production. \nPurchasing and marketing of organic products \n†19. SHRI BHAGAT SINGH KOSHYARI: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether any proposal for purchasing and marketing of organic farm \nproducts to encourage organic farming is under Government's \nconsideration; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) to (c) There is no proposal under Government's consideration \nfor purchasing and marketing of organic farm products as the Government \nis already promoting organic farming and the demand for organic produce \nis growing substantially. \nAgriculture research products \n†20. DR. MURLI MANOHAR JOSHI: \n SHRI RAM JETHMALANI: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the work is being carried out on various \nresearch projects in agricultural research institutes and in other \nresearch institutes of public sector in the country; \n(b) if so, foodgrain-wise number of these projects; \n(c) by when these projects are likely to be completed indicating the \ntime since when the same were started; and \n(d) the details of the average annual expenditure made on these \nprojects? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Seven Centrally Sponsored All India Coordinated Research Projects, \none each in Wheat and Barley, Rice, Sorghum, Maize, Pearl Millet, Small \nMillet and four in Pulses (Chickpea, Pigeon Pea, MULLARP, and Arid \nLegumes) are currently in operation. In addition 19 projects on cereals \n\n141 \nand pulses and three other projects on value chain enrichment and \npopularization of potential foodgrains are being funded under NAIP which \nare in operation in different State Agricultural Universities and other \npublic Research Institutes. \n(c) Project-wise date of start of All India Coordinated Research \nProjects on foodgrains is as follows:- \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n142 \nSl.No. Foodgrains Date of Start       Likely \ndate of  \n                \ncompletion \n1. Wheat and Barley (AICRP, Karnal) 1965 continued \n2. Rice (AICRP, Hyderabad) 1965 continued \n3. Sorghum (AICRP, Hyderabad) 1969 continued \n4. Maize (AICRP, Delhi) 1957 continued \n5. Pearl Millet (AICRP, Mandor) 1986 continued \n6. Small Millet (AICRP, Mangalore) 1986 continued \n7. Pulses (AICRP) 1966/1993 continued \nThe NAIP projects started in the year 2006- 07. The All India \nCoordinated Projects are operational for the period upto XIth Plan and \nNAIP Projects are likely to be completed in 2012. \n(d) The annual expenditure based on last three years average on these \nprojects is as follows:- \nAll India Coordinated Research Projects Wheat and Barley, Rs.556.25 \nlakh, All India Coordinated Research Projects Rice, Rs.636.84 lakh, All \nIndia Coordinated Research Projects Sorghum, Rs.287.36 lakh, All India \nCoordinated Research Projects Maize, Rs.453.51 lakh, All India \nCoordinated Research Projects Pearl Millet, Rs.283.02 lakh, All India \nCoordinated Research Projects Small Millet, Rs.376.69 lakh, All India \nCoordinated Research Projects (Pulses) [Chickpea, MULLARP, Pigeon Pea, \nand Arid Legumes], Rs. 1083.00 lakh. \nThe annual budget (2008- 09) for the Projects under NAIP is Rs.1895.6 \nlakhs \nPrice hike of agricultural instruments \n†21. SHRI PYARELAL KHANDELWAL: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that the prices of agricultural instruments \nhave increased during the last one year; \n(b) if so, the names of the implements that have become costlier; and \n(c) the details of steps undertaken by Government to reduce the prices \nof agricultural instruments? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) and (b) Data on price rise of individual agricultural \n\n143 \nimplements is not available, due to diversity of manufacturers as also \nthe very wide variety of types and sizes of agricultural implements. The \nprices are governed by market forces and vary from State to State and \narea to area within the States. \n(c) The Government has taken measures such as waiving of Excise duty \non all agricultural implements and provision of subsidy to farmers for \nthe purchase of different agricultural equipments under various \nGovernment schemes/programmes. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n144 \nRice varieties with more arsenic \n22. SHRI N.K. SINGH: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether it is a fact that CSIR has found some rice varieties which \naccumulate much more arsenic from the soil with the result that their \nconsumption is dangerous for human health; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) whether any steps are contemplated to counter the arsenic effect? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) and (b) The studies conducted by National Botanical Research \nInstitute, Lucknow under Council of Scientific and Industrial Research \n(CSIR) have found rice germplasms such as BRG- 12, BRG- 15 and BRG- 20 \naccumulating higher arsenic (range 1034 ug 2650 ug/kg grain rice). \nCertain rice germplasms accumulating lower arsenic level were also \nidentified: CN-1646-2, CN-1646-5, Gotrabhog and Nayanmoni (range 78.65 ug \nto 112.26 fig/kg grain rice). \n(c) The Indian Council of Agricultural Research (ICAR) had taken up \nresearch on mitigation options of arsenic problem in West Bengal. Growing \nvarious legumes and pulses need to be encouraged which require less \nground water. There should be more use of organic and green manures that \nmoderate arsenic toxicity in soil-plant system by forming oregano-arsenic \ncomplexes. The Blue Green Algae (BGA) having ability to decontaminate \nsoils of arsenic should be introduced in the pad dy fields. To have more \nbasic and strategic research in arsenic related problems in entire food \nchain, a project under National Agricultural Innovation Project (NAIP) \ninvolving six centres with Bidhan Chandra Krishi Vishwa Vidyalaya \n(BCKVV), Mohanpur as lead centre has been initiated for a period of more \nthan 5 years with a budget of Rs.6.24 crores. \nLaw for protection of bovine species \n23. SHRI RAJKUMAR DHOOT: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether Government has made any law for protection of bovine \nspecies; \n(b) if, so the details therefor; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n\n145 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI \nTASLIMUDDIN): (a) and (b) No, Sir. However, Government of India has \ninitiated a major programme National Project for Cattle and Buffalo \nBreeding (NPCBB) in October 2000 in two phases of five years each. NPCBB \nenvisages genetic upgradation of bovine population on priority basis and \nalso has focus on development and conservation of indigenous breeds. In \naddition, the Government of India is also implementing Central Sector \nSchemes viz. Central Cattle Breeding Farms (CCBFs), Central Frozen Semen \nProduction and Training Institute (CFSP&TI) and Central Herd Registration \nScheme (CHRS) for the development of bovine population . \n\n146 \n(c) The issues related to law for protection of bovine species come \nunder the purview of the State legislature. Some of the States have \nalready passed legislations on protection of bovine species. \nAgro-research in the country \n†24. SHRI RAJ MOHINDER SINGH MAJITHA: \n SHRI SHIVANAND TIWARI: \nWill the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that research work is going on in Government \nsector institutes to enhance the quality and production of agro- products \nin the country; \n(b) if so, the number of institutes engaged in Agro- research in the \ncountry by the end of December, 2008; \n(c) the break-up of personnel engaged in research work, administrative \nwork and services in these institutes; and \n(d) the amount spent on these institutes during 2004-05 and 2007-08? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) Yes, Sir. \n(b) Under the Indian Council of Agricultural Research, 95 institutes \nare engaged in agro- research including 48 National/Central Institutes, 6 \nNational Bureaux, 22 National Research Centres (NRCs) and 19 Project \nDirectorates (PDs). \n(c) The number of personnel engaged in research work, administrative \nwork and services in the institutes including ICAR headquarter is as \nfollows:- \n   Scientists   4165 \n   Technical   6478 \n   Administrative  4440 \n   Supporting  8292 \n(d) The amount spent on institutes during 2004-05 and 2007-08:- \n(Rs. in Crore) \n Plan Non-plan \n2004-05 858.98 788.37 \n2007-08 1284.26 948.95 \nNeed for a second green revolution \n25. SHRI KAMAL AKHTAR: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased to \nstate: \n\n147 \n(a) whether a report by Associated Chamber of Commerce and Industry of \nIndia (ASSOCHAM) has recommended that second Green Revolution is called \nfor the produce high yielding of agricultural products to meet the \ngrowing demand; \n\n148 \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether the report points out that the impact of a neglected \nagriculture sector have a long term impact and the yield of wheat and \nrice growth rates have shown little progress; and \n(d) if so, the steps taken by Government to enhance the production of \nfoodgrains and pulses? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) to (c) The Associated Chamber of Commerce and Industry of \nIndia (ASSOCHAM) held a summit on Green Revolution II with focus on \nknowledge Agriculture at New Delhi from 22-24 November, 2006. The overall \nobjective of the summit was to evolve a strategy for propelling Indian \nAgriculture on a high growth trajectory. The summit noted the rapid \nprogress being achieved in the field of Agriculture in India. The \nunanimous view at the summit was that to be a global leader in \nagriculture, India must promote opportunities for technology development, \nknowledge creation and market access amongst all the players in the \nAgriculture sector. \nThere has been record production of food grains of 230.67 million \ntones during 2007- 08 (4th Advance Estimates). All time high record of \nproduction of Wheat 78.40 millions tones and rice 96.43 millions tones \nhas been achieved during 2007-08 (4th Advance Estimates). \n(d) Indian Council of Agricultural Research / Department of \nAgricultural Research and Education has undertaken research for \ndeveloping a number a number of varieties and hybrid in food crops along \nwith production and protection technologies. The Government of India has \nalso launched National Food Security Mission for enhancing the production \nand productivity of rice, wheat and pulses. \nSupply of fertilizers in Andhra Pradesh \n26. SHRI MOHD. ALI KHAN: Will the Minister of CHEMICALS AND \nFERTILIZERS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether farmers are getting fertilizers in time in each State \nespecially in Andhra Pradesh; \n(b) if so, the details thereof during the last three years; and \n(c) if not, the reasons therefor? \n\n149 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF CHEMICALS AND FERTILIZERS \n(SHRI B.K. HANDIQUE): (a) to (c) Yes, Sir. Urea is the only fertilizer \nwhich is under partial movement and distribution control of the \nGovernment. Union Government ensures availability of urea at State level. \nState Governments are responsible for its distribution within the State. \nAll other fertilizers viz. DAP, MOP, SSP and NPK etc. are decontrolled/ \nde-canalized since 1992. The availability of Phosphatic and Potassic \nfertilizers is decided by the market forces of demand and supply. The gap \nbetween requirement and indigenous production is met through imports. The \navailability and sales of Urea, DAP and MOP in Andhra Pradesh during the \nlast three years has been as under: \n\n150 \n(LMT) \nProduct                2005-06                 2006-07 \n                   2007-08 \n Availability Sales Availability Sales Availability Sales \nUrea 23.53 22.18 24.52 22.13 26.84 25.12 \nDAP 6.12 5.96 6.15 5.94 7.16 7.15 \nMOP 4.61 4.13 3.90 3.72 4.52 4.50 \nState-wise availability and sales of Urea, DAP and MOP during last \nthree years is also given in the statement. \nStatement \nState-wise requirement, availability and sale of Urea, DAP  \nand MOP during the three years \n2005-06 \nProduct  Urea   DAP   MOP (LMTs) \nName of StateRequire-Availa- SalesRequire-Availa- SalesRequire-Availa-\n Sales \n ment bility  ment bility  ment bility  \nAndhra Pradesh 23.50 23.53 22.18 7.05 6.12 5.96 3.86 4.61 4.13 \nKarnataka 9.55 11.03 10.67 4.60 5.00 4.57 2.85 3.79 3.36 \nKerala 1.55 1.26 1.18 0.20 0.17 0.16 1.30 1.29 1.19 \nTamil Nadu 9.50 9.06 8.78 3.50 3.87 3.64 4.50 3.76 3.67 \nGujarat 12.50 13.81 13.26 5.20 6.65 5.41 1.30 1.89 1.55 \nMadhya Pradesh 11.55 10.97 10.20 5.95 5.76 4.66 0.50 0.91 0.57 \nChhattisgarh 4.70 4.67 4.45 1.16 1.38 1.27 0.49 0.79 0.49 \nMaharashtra 16.75 18.19 17.21 5.85 5.84 5.13 2.45 2.92 2.47 \nRajasthan 12.30 12.35 11.59 4.90 5.41 4.14 0.20 0.32 0.17 \nHaryana 16.00 18.00 16.44 5.60 6.64 5.10 0.30 0.55 0.36 \nPunjab 24.70 24.55 23.72 7.65 9.54 7.32 0.65 1.25 0.85 \nUttar Pradesh 52.00 50.36 45.95 15.50 15.89 12.34 2.60 3.01 1.66 \nUttaranchal 1.77 2.01 1.75 0.27 0.31 0.23 0.11 0.07 0.05 \nJammu &  1.20 1.30 1.11 0.70 0.65 0.56 0.17 0.09 0.07 \nKashmir \nBihar 14.30 14.24 7.32 3.00 2.12 1.78 1.40 1.64. 1.23 \nJharkhand 1.75 1.56 1.51 1.16 0.73 0.68 0.26 0.05 0.05 \nOrissa 4.75 4.31 1.09 0.90 0.92 0.81 1.0