kely to be  \n     completed during 2009-\n10. \n6. Sambalpur- 474.25 0.61 65.00 The work is being \n Titlagarh    executed by RVNL \n (182 kms)    through ADB funding. \nFLS  \n     has been completed.  \n     Necessary planning has \nbeen  \n     taken up. \n7. Raipur- 614.35 0.00 65.00 The work is being \nexecuted  \n Titlagarh    by RVNL through ADB  \n (203 kms)    funding. Necessary \nplanning  \n     has been taken up. \n8. Padapahar- 129.74 30.61 70.00 Padapahar-Dongaposi \n Banspani    has been completed. \n (28 Kms)    Work on balance section \nis  \n     likely to be completed \nduring  \n     2009-10. \n9. Bimlagarh- 73.77 0.16 50.00 Detailed Estimate has \n Dumitra    been sanctioned. \n (18.3 Kms)    Tendering for works has  \n     been taken up. The \nproject  \n     is likely to be \ncompleted  \n\n277 \n     during 2010-11. \n10. Barbil- 50.01 1.32 40.00 Detailed Estimate has \n Barjamda    been sanctioned. \nEarthwork  \n (10 Kms)    and bridgework  have \nbeen  \n     taken up. The project \nis  \n     likely to be completed \nduring  \n     2009-10. \n11. Banspani- 76.22 – 4.00 Detailed Estimate has \n Joruli    been sanctioned. \n (9 Kms)    Tendering for works has  \n     been taken up. \nThe ongoing projects will be completed in the coming years as per the \navailability of resources. \n\n278 \nSurvey of new railway lines in Rajasthan \n†88. SHRI LALIT KISHORE CHATURVEDI: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) the number of proposals for survey of new railway lines in \nRajasthan have been included in Rail Budget 2008-09; \n(b) the details of the progress made of in earlier proposed survey \nworks, survey-wise; \n(c) the rank of Rajasthan in comparison to other States as far as rail \nnetworking is concerned; and \n(d) whether the survey work for new railway lines of Jaipur- Malpura-\nKekdi-Shahpura-Bheelwara-Gangapur-Nathdwara, Nagaur- Jayal-Ladnu-Sikar-\nNeem Ka Thana and Jaisalmar-Badmer would be carried out? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nThree. \n(b) and (d) A Statement is enclosed. \n(c) Rajasthan ranks second among all States. \nStatement \nDetailed Surveys conducted/completed since 2004-05 in the State of \nRajasthan \nSl. Name of the project Year of KMS \nNo.  Inclusion/ \n  Completion \n1           2 3 4 \nA. Surveys completed and project sanctioned for execution. \n1. Loharu-Sikar-Churu-Ringus-Jaipur Gauge Conversion. 06-07 320 \n2. Udaipur City-Himmatnagar-Ahmedabad Gauge Conversion. 06-07 299 \n3. Jaipur-Dausa Doubling. 04-05 61 \n4. Sadulpur-Ratangarh-Bikaner & Ratangarh-Degana Gauge 04-05 364 \n Conversion. \n5. Dausa-Bandikui Gauge Conversion. 04-05 90 \n6. Suratpura-Hannumangarh Gauge Conversion. 07-08 240 \nB. Surveys completed and project proposal could not be considered  \nbecause of heavy throwforward & unremunerative nature. \n1. Ratlam-Banswara via Dungarpur New Line. 04-05 176 \n2. Phalodi-Balotra via Shergarh, Shaitrawa & Dechhu New Line.04-05 165 \n3. Phulera to Merta Road Doubling. 07-08 153 \n\n279 \n4. Rewari-Bhiwadi New Line. 04-05 27 \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n280 \n1           2 3 4 \n5. Ban Sadari-Nimach New Line. 06-07 50 \n6. Dahod-Banswara New Line. 04-05 120 \n7. Loharu-Bhiwani New Line. 06-07 64 \n8. Ajmer-Kota New Line. 07-08 145 \n9. Rail Link to Tonk-Devli New Line. 07-08 62 \n10. Jaisalmer-Kandla New Line. 05-06 562 \n11. Anupgarh-Kolayat via Khajuwala New Line. 07-08 200 \n12. Anupgarh-Khajuwala-Jaisalmer-Ramgarh New Line. 07-08 485 \n13. Jaisalmer-Barmer New Line. 05-06 144 \n14. Ujjain-Jhalawar/Ramgarnjmandi New Line (Updating). 07-08 190 \nC. Surveys completed. \n1. Pushkar-Merta road New Line. 07-08 59 \n2. Bilara-Bar New Line. 06-07 52 \n3. Anupgarh - Bikaner New Line. 04-05 155 \n4. Phalodi-Nagaur New Line. 06-07 147 \n5. Mavli-Marwar Gauge Conversion. 07-08 183 \n6. Mavali to Bari Sadari Gauge Conversion. 08-09 82 \n7. Gwalior-Shivpur Kalan Gauge Conversion and ext. up to Kota05-06 200 \n New Line (Updating). \n8. Ratlam-Banswara-Dungarpur New Line (Updating). 07-08 176 \n9. Rewari-Bhiwadi New Line(Updating). 08-09 27 \nD. Survey taken up. \n1. Dholpur-Sirmuttra Gauge Conversion (Updating). 04-05 144 \n2. Himmatnagar-Khedbranhma Gauge Conversion with ext. up 07-08 120 \n to Abu road. \n3. Bina-Kota Doubling. 07-08 283 \n4. Amarpura (Jorasi) to Chirawa via Tathwari and Singhana 08-09 70 \n New Line. \n5. Rewari-Palwal-Khurja New Line. 08-09 115 \n6. Jhunjhunu-Pilani New Line. 06-07 17 \n(d) Jaipur to Nathdwara new line via Gangapur-Bhilwara- Shahpura-\nKekri- \n\n281 \nMalpura. \n\n282 \nA survey for a new BG line from Jaipur to Nathdwara via Diggi-Malpura-\nKekri-Shahpura-Banera Bhilwara-Gangapur (350 kms) was conducted in the \npast but the project could not be taken up keeping in view its \nunremunerative nature and constraint of resources. \nJaisalmer to Barmer New Line. \nThis is a part of Jaisalmer- Kandla new broad gauge line for which \nsurvey has been done. Work could not be taken up due to large \nthrowforward of ongoing projects, constraint of resources and \nunremunerative nature of the line. \nNagaur-Javal-Ladnu-Sikar-Neem Ka Thana New Line. \nNo survey has been done. \nAccident on Saryu Express \n†89. SHRI MOTTLAL VORA: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government is aware that on 19th January, 2009, a large \nnumber of youth boarded the roofs of coaches because of non- availability \nof seats in Saryu Express and fifty youths fell down from the roofs at \nChilbila station; \n(b) if so, the number of youth died and injured; \n(c) the aid extended by Government to the bereaved families of the \ndead and injured; \n(d) the reasons for not stopping the youth from boarding the roofs of \nthe coaches; and \n(e) the steps being taken by Government to prevent such accidents in \nfuture? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nYes, Sir. On 19.01.2009, some persons were found travelling on the roof \nof Train No.4125 (Saryu Express) at Pratapgarh Railway station and were \nbrought down and accommodated inside the coaches by Railway Protection \nForce and Government Railway Police. On arrival of the train at Chilbila \nRailway station, few persons travelling unauthorizedely again boarded the \nroof of the train. Some of them fell down from the roof due to hit by \nbranch of a tree near the track when the train was passing between \nChilbila and Khandor Railway stations in Pratapgarh- Sultanpur section \nover Lucknow Division of Northern Railway. \n(b) 3 persons died and 28 were injured in this incident. \n\n283 \n(c) No financial aid is permissible to the dependents of those dead \nand injured in this accident, as traveling on roof top of a train is \nunauthorized and is punishable offence under the provisions of the \nRailways Act, 1989. \n(d) Efforts were made to detrain the unauthorized roof travelers at \nPratapgarh and Chilbila stations by Railway Protection Force Government \nRailway Police and station staff, but some of the youth climbed again on \nthe roof of the compartment when train left from Chilbila, where there \nwas no deployment of Railway Protection Force / Government Railway Police \nStaff. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n284 \n(e) Following steps are being taken to prevent such incidents in \nfuture:- \n1. Frequent drives are being conducted by Railway Protection Force \nagainst roof travellers to prevent such incidents. \n2. Escorting staff are being briefed regularly to check such incidents \nduring train escorting duty. \n3. Supervisory staff are also conducting frequent surprise checks to \nprevent such incidents. \n4. Action is being taken under section 156 of the Railways Act against \nthe roof travellers. \n5. Security personnel have been advised to be more vigilant to prevent \nsuch incidents in future. \n6. Railway Protection Force, Government Railway Police and Commercial \nstaff, where available, are advised not to allow any person to board \non the roof of the train. \nUnused railway land \n†90. SHRI JAI PARKASH AGGARWAL: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether his Ministry proposes to give its unused land to the State \nGovernments at their request for introducing new schemes and developing \nthe same; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; \n(c) the extent of such land comes under each State; \n(d) whether Government proposes to give this unused land on lease to \nthe families living thereon; and \n(e) if so, the details thereof? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nto (c) No, Sir. The vacant railway land is required by Railways for its \nfuture development works. However, till such time, the land is required \nby Railways for its own operational use, the same is put to various \nshort-term uses like commercial licensing, commercial plantation, Grow \nMore Food (GMF) scheme, etc. In addition, where potential exists it is \nproposed to undertake commercial development of land through Rail Land \n\n285 \nDevelopment Authority (RLDA). \n(d) No, Sir. \n(e) Does not arise. \nManufacturing capacity during eleventh five year plan \n91. SHRI LALIT KISHORE CHATURVEDI: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) the  targets  set  for  manufacturing  of  wagons,  coaches, \nelectric engines, diesel engines,  \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n286 \nEMU, MMU, DMU during the Eleventh Five Year Plan and the names of places \nwhere they are being manufactured at present and their manufacturing \ncapacity; \n(b) whether any plan has been prepared to augment the manufacturing \ncapacity of these units; and \n(c) whether participation of private sector would sought for their \nmanufacturing? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nThe targets set for manufacturing of Wagons, Coaches, Electric E ngines, \nDiesel Engines, EMU, MEMU, DMU during the 11th Five Year Plan are as \nunder:- \nRolling Stock Target \nWagons 62,000 \nCoaches 17,500 \nElectric Engines 1,800 \nDiesel Engines 1,800 \nElectric Multiple Unit (EMU), Mainline Electric 5,000 \nMultiple Unit (MEMU), Diesel Multiple Unit(DMU) \nThe place of manufacture and capacity of the rolling stock mentioned \nabove are as follows:- \nRolling Stock                     Location                        \nCapacity \n1 Coaches, EMU, MEMU, Integral Coach Factory, Chennai 1000 Coaches \n DMU Rail Coach Factory, Kapurthala 1000 Coaches \n2. Electric Locomotives Chittaranjan Locomotive Works, 150 Locomotives \n  Chittaranjan \n3. Diesel Locomotives Diesel Locomotive Works, Varanasi 150 \nLocomotives \nOther than these, coaches are also being manufactured by M/s BEML, \nBangalore in the Public Sector. Orders have also been placed for \nmanufacturing of EMUs on manufacturers in the Public/Private Sector \nthrough the open tendering mode. Wagons are being manufactured in 5 \nRailway Workshops, 6 Public Sector Units and 7 Private Sector Units. In \n2007-08, approximately 15000 wagons were manufactured by these 18 Units. \nThe existing manufacturing capacity of wagons per annum in Railway \nWorkshops is as follows:- \ni) Jamalpur Workshop - 120 wagons ii) Samastipur Workshop-  300 wagons \n\n287 \niii) Amritsar Workshop -300 wagons iv) Golden Rock Workshop, Tamil Nadu - \n450 wagons and v) Hubli Workshop- 120 wagons. \n(b) Yes, Sir. Works have been sanctioned to increase the installed \nproduction capacities of the Railway Production Units/Workshops as \nmentioned below:- \n• Integral Coach Factory, Chennai and Rail Coach Factory, Kapurthala: \n1000 coaches to 1 500 coaches per annum each. \n\n288 \n• Chittaranjan Locomotive Works, Chittaranjan and Diesel Locomotive \nWorks, Varanasi: 150 locomotives to 200 locomotives per annum each. \n• Railway Workshops for Wagon Manufacturing: i) Jamalpur Workshop -  120 \nto 540 wagons ii) Golden Rock Workshop, Tamil Nadu - 450 to 800 wagons \nand iii) Hubli Workshop- 120 to 180 wagons. \n(c) Private participation is being sought in Greenfield Manufacturing \nUnits being set up at Marhowra for Diesel Locomotives and Madhepura for \nElectric Locomotives. \nTenders for dedicated corridors \n†92. SHRI SHREEGOPAL VYAS: \n SHRI BALAVANT ALIAS BAL APTE: \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether tenders for dedicated corridors have been received; \n(b) the names of places where such systems exist in the world; and \n(c) the details of the number of corridors proposed to be constructed \nand the time by when they are targeted to be completed? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nTenders for construction work on Dedicated Freight Corridors have been \nreceived on the following two Sections:- \n(i) Sonnagar- Mugalsarai ( Eastern Corridor) \n(ii) Major and minor bridges between Vaitarana- Surat (Western \nCorridor). \n(b) Many countries have been running heavy haul and long haul freight \ntrains on dedicated freight lines. Major projects are under operation in \nUnited States of America (USA), Canada, Brazil, South Africa, Australia \nand New Zealand. \n(c) The Government of India has approved the construction of Dedicated \nFreight Corridor Projects on Eastern Route (Ludhiana-Dankuni) and Western \nRoute (Jawaharlal Nehru Port near Mumbai to Dadri/Tughlakabad). They are \ntargeted for completion in about 5 years from the start of construction \nof the Project. \nPrivate investment in new railway lines \n93. SHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) whether Railways have agreed to rope in private players to improve \nfreight connectivity; \n(b) if so, whether the models allow private players for the first tune \nto develop tracks on their own and avail freight rebate on traffic; \n(c) whether Railways would acquire land, the cost of which would have \nto be paid by the participants; \n(d) whether Railways have finalized the policy for investment in link \n\n289 \nlines with two options, advance contribution model and SPV; \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n290 \n(e) whether there has been a good response from the private sector; \nand \n(f) by when final decision would be taken for private investment in \nnew railway lines ? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nand (b) Yes, Sir. This is possible in the Advance Contribution Model \nwhere the party contributes 100% of the project cost. \n(c) Yes, Sir. \n(d) Yes, Sir. \n(e) No firm proposal has been received so far. \n(f) Does not arise. \nPassenger halt at Nalco Nagar \n†94. SHRI RUDRA NARAYAN PANY: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether his Ministry is aware of the fact that there has been a \nyear long demand for setting up a passenger halt at Nalco Nagar under the \nEast-Coast Railway of Angul district in Orissa; \n(b) if so, his Ministry's approach in this regard and whether there is \nany proposal for setting up a passenger halt at that place in near \nfuture; \n(c) whether it is also a fact that a public undertaking, National \nAluminium Company (NALCO) itself had proposed to carry out the work of \nsetting up platform; and \n(d) if so, by when the same would be set up? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nYes, Sir. \n(b) to (d) National Aluminium Company's commitment to bear cost of \nconstruction has been received recently by East Coast Railway. The \nopening of halt will be considered if found financially viable after \nexamination. \nAssistance to beggars engaged in disposal of passengers died in accidents \n95. SHRI N.K. SINGH: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to \nstate: \n(a) whether Government is aware that beggar families living near \n\n291 \nrailway tracks in Ghaziabad are engaged in disposal of persons/ \npassengers who die in accidents on railway tracks from the money earned \nfrom begging on Saturdays; and \n(b) whether Government proposes to assist them financially and grant \nrecognition to their deeds of social service? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nNo, Sir. \n(b) Does not arise. \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n292 \nPublic-private partnership for port connectivity \n96. SHRI B. J. PANDA: \n MS. MABEL REBELLO: \nWill the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether India Inc. has shown interest to work as partners with \nRailways for port connectivity; \n(b) if so, the details thereof; and \n(c) what is the status of this project in the State of Orissa, \nindicating the details of such projects that are proposed to be executed \nin this region in Public-Private Partnership mode? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nand (b) Yes, Sir. India Inc. has shown interest in developing rail \nconnectivity to Dhamra, Kirtania, Paradip ports on the East Coast and \nDighi, Dahej, Hazira, Rewas ports on the West Coast. \n(c) Currently, work is in progress on the Haridaspur -  Paradip new \nrailway line for providing additional connectivity to Paradip Port. This \nis being implemented by Rail Vikas Nigam Limited through a project \nspecific Special Purpose Vehicle. \nConstruction of over/under rail bridges in Punjab \n97. SHRI VARINDER SINGH BAJWA: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) the number of proposals for constructions of over/under rail \nbridges sought by Punjab Government and local bodies etc. in the State, \nstill pending for clearance; and \n(b) the number out of them likely to be cleared during 2009- 10 with \nthe likely period to be taken for their completion in each case? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nIn all 22 proposals were received out of which 17 are on cost sharing \nbasis & remaining 5 on deposit terms from Punjab Government. \n(b) The works are taken as sanctioned only after passing of Railway \nBudget by Parliament. In case of cost sharing works, Railways construct \nbridge proper across tracks and approaches are constructed by State Road \nauthority. Hence completion depends upon completion of both side's works. \nRailways generally complete their portion ahead of approaches work or \nsimultaneously with it. For deposit works execution is taken up after \ndeposition of cost by State Govt. Approximately 3 years are taken in \ncompletion of works after commencement of construction work. \n\n293 \nMaintenance of stations and trains in Bihar \n98. SHRI SABIR ALI: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that maintenance of stations and trains in \nBihar is not up to the mark; \n(b) the details of surprise inspections carried out by senior officers \nduring the last six months in Bihar and the results thereof; \n\n294 \n(c) the remedial measures taken; and \n(d) by when those shortcomings would be rectified? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nNo, Sir. \n(b) to (d) Maintenance of the stations and trains on Indian Railways \nincluding those in Bihar State is a continuous process. Inspections \nincluding surprise inspections are done on regular basis by Divisional \nand Headquarters officers under extant instructions. Deficiencies noted \nduring the inspections are complied by the concerned officials as early \nas possible through departmental/ contractual agencies. Wherever \nconsidered necessary, upgradation works are sanctioned through regular \nWorks Programme of Railways. Damage and pilferage of coach fittings, that \nsome time happen during run, are made good during periodical maintenance. \nNight vision goggles and cameras \n99. SHRI VIJAY JAWAHARLAL DARDA: Will the Minister of RAILWAYS be \npleased to state: \n(a) what is the result of the experiment of 'night- vision goggles and \ncameras' to make drivers see signals and tracks in zero- visibility \nconditions; \n(b) what is the envisaged degree of reliability regarding 'Image \nAmplification' technique, which is innovative in nature; and \n(c) whether it is Railways' own indigenous technique or it is being \nimported? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF RAILWAYS (SHRI R. VELU): (a) \nNo such experiment with night vision goggles and cameras to make the \ndrivers see signals and track in zero visibility conditions has been \nmade. \n(b) and (c) Do not arise. \nWithdrawal of exemption of import of TMT \n100. DR. T. SUBBARAMI REDDY: \n SHRI GIREESH KUMAR SANGHI; \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n(a) whether Government has withdrawn the exemption of import of TMT \nwhich provides relief to domestic manufacturers of the items used for the \nconstruction sector; \n\n295 \n(b) if so, whether steel companies ramp up output as demand rises; \n(c) if so, whether the Steel Authority of India Ltd. has taken the \nslow down of steel seriously and has pointed out that Government needs to \ndo more to see that the steel industry remains in shape; \n(d) if so, whether action taken so far indicate that Government has \nnot appreciated the depth of the problem; and \n(e) if so, the measures Government is considering to help the steel \nindustry? \n\n296 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF STEEL (SHRI JITIN PRASADA): \n(a) Yes, Sir. The Government has re- imposed countervailing duty (CVD) on \nimport of TMT bars and structurals w.e.f. 2.1.2009. \n(b) CVD on import of TMT bars and structurals was withdrawn as one of \nthe measures' to control inflation and contain the rising steel prices. \nThe production of various categories of steel, including TMT bars and \nstructurals by the steel producers, is determined by the demand of that \nparticular category in the market. \n(c) In view of slowdown in steel sector, Steel Authority of India \nLimited along with other steel producers has approached the Government \nfor taking suitable measures to stimulate demand of steel in the country \nand provide relief to the domestic steel industry which has been badly \naffected due to severe downturn in the global economy leading to a \nsituation of excess availability of steel in the market and slow off take \nfrom consuming sectors. \n(d) and (e) The Government is closely monitoring the situation and is \nin continuous dialogue with the steel industry. The following measures \nhave already been taken by the Government in view of the current \ndownturn: \n(i) Export Duty on all steel items (except melting scrap) withdrawn \nw.e.f. 31.10.2008. \n(ii) DEPB on steel items restored w.e.f. 14.11.2008.  \n(iii) Import Duty on iron and non- alloy steel items re- imposed at 5%, \nw.e.f. 18.11.2008. \n(iv) 'Hot Rolled Coil' brought into restricted category of import w.e.f.  \n21.11.08 \n(v) CENVAT on steel items reduced from 14% to 10% w.e.f. 7.12.2008. \n(vi) Countervailing duty (CVD) on TMT bars and structurals reintroduced \nw.e.f. 2.1.2009. \nFuture trade in steel \n101. SHRI R.C. SINGH: Will the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n(a) the details of iron-ore reserves in the country, State-wise; \n(b) the details of iron- ore reserves under the control of Government \nand the private sector, State-wise; \n(c) whether it is not a fact that small and medium iron- ore \nmanufacturers are facing problems in view of futures trade in steel; and \n(d) if so, how Government is planning to address the concerns of small \nand medium iron-ore manufactures in the country? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF STEEL (SHRI JITIN PRASADA): \n(a) The details of iron ore reserves in the country, State-wise are given \nbelow: \n\n297 \n(As on 1.4.2005) \nMineral Unit State Reserve \n1 2   3 4 \nIron Ore (Heamatite) 1000 Tonnes Andhra Pradesh 39,596 \n  Chhattisgarh 760,512 \n  Goa 458,703 \n\n298 \n1 2   3 4 \n  Jharkhand 2,494,424 \n  Karnataka 940,429 \n  Madhya Pradesh 33,917 \n  Maharashtra 13,997 \n  Orissa 2,251,777 \n  Rajasthan 10,813 \n TOTAL  7,004,168 \nIron Ore (Magnetite) 1000 Tonnes Goa 50,112 \n  Jharkhand 3,390 \n  Maharashtra 621 \n  Orissa 156 \n  Rajasthan 4,225 \n TOTAL  58,503 \n(SOURCE: Indian Bureau of Mines (IBM), Ministry of Mines, Government of India)  \n(b) The details of iron ore reserves in public sector leasehold and \nprivate sector leasehold as on 1.4.2005 are given below: \n(As on 1.4.2005) \nMineral Unit State Name Owner Reserves \n1 2 3 4 5 \nIron Ore (Heamatite) 1000 Tonnes ChhattisgarhPrivate\n 1,388 \n   Public 759,124 \n  Goa Private 442,543 \n  Jharkhand Private 214,151 \n   Public 2,280,272 \n  Karnataka Private 706,031 \n   Public 228,631 \n  Madhya Pradesh Private 31,764 \n  Maharashtra Private 13,997 \n  Orissa Private 523,958 \n   Public 1,327,819 \n  Rajasthan Private 10,813 \n Total   6,980,086 \nIron Ore (Magnetite) 1000 Tonnes Goa Private\n 50,112 \n  Jharkhand Private 908 \n\n299 \n   Public 2,483 \n\n300 \n1 2 3 4 5 \n  Maharashtra Private 89 \n   Public 532 \n  Orissa Private 156 \n  Rajasthan Private 4,225 \n TOTAL   58,504 \n(SOURCE: Indian Bureau of Mines (IBM), Ministry of Mines, Government of India) \n(c) and (d) No such representation regarding problems being faced by \nthe small and medium iron ore manufacturers in view of futures trade in \nsteel has been received in Ministry of Steel. \nDemand criris of steel due to economic recession \n102. SHRI RAMDAS AGARWAL: \n SHRI OM PRAKASH MATHUR: \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n(a) whether public and private sectors in steel are facing demand \ncrisis due to economic recession in world; \n(b) if so, the gap between demand and supply of steel during the years \n2006-07, 2007-08 and 2008-09; \n(c) the target set by Government for production of steel during the \nEleventh Five Year Plan; \n(d) whether Government intends to augment the production capacity of \nexisting steel plants as well as propose to set up new plants in the  \ncountry particularly in Orissa; \n(e) if so, the details thereof; and \n(f) if not, the reasons therefor? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF STEEL (SHRI JITIN PRASADA): \n(a) Overall steel consumption in the country has recorded a decline since \nOctober 2008 of the current financial year, due to adverse effect of \nglobal economic crisis in the domestic steel market. \n(b) The gap between demand (consumption) and supply (production for \nsale) of finished steel in the country during the years 2006- 07, 2007-08 \nand April-December 2008-09 is given in the table below:- \nYear                                          Total Finished Steel (unit: \nmillion tonnes) \n Production for sale Consumption Gap \n (Supply) (Demand) \n2006-07 52.53 46.78 5.75 \n\n301 \n2007-08 56.07 52.12 3.95 \nApril-Dec. 2008-09* 42.08 37.84 14.24 \nSOURCE: Join Plant Committee, * = provisional \n\n302 \nThe gap between supply and consumption is bridged by way of export and \nimport of steel, depending upon net demand and surplus/deficit in \ndomestic availability. \n(c) As per the Eleventh Five Year Plan (2007- 2012) document, the \nestimated production of crude steel in the country is likely to be around \n80 million tonnes by the end of Plan period i.e. by 2011-12. \n(d) to (f) Indian steel sector is de- regulated and Government is not \ndirectly involved in production of steel. However, Government is acting \nas a facilitator through its various policy initiatives towards growth \nand sustainability of steel sector in the country. The public sector \nsteel units namely Steel Authority of India Limited (SAIL), Rashtriya \nIspat Nigam Limited (RINL) and National Mineral Development Corporation \nLimited (NMDC) have undertaken capacity expansion, modernization and \nGreenfield projects. SAIL has undertaken capacity expansion and \nmodernization of its plants to augment its crude steel production \ncapacity to nearly 25 million tonnes. Similarly, RINL is also expanding \nits capacity to 6.3 million tonnes. NMDC Limited is setting up a 3.0 \nmillion tonnes capacity Integrated Steel Plant in Chhattisgarh. \nIn Orissa, SAIL has undertaken modernization and expansion of its \nRourkela Steel Plant to increase hot metal production capacity from 2.12 \nmillion tonnes to 4.5 million tonnes. \nNew payscales for workers in refectiories  \n†103. SHRI SHREEGOPAL VYAS: \n  SHRI BALAVANT ALIAS BAL APTE: \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n(a) whether workers in refectories factories under the Steel Ministry \nare being given new pay scales; \n(b) if so, since when; and \n(c) if not, the conditions on fulfillment of which the same will be \ngiven? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF STEEL (SHRI JITIN PRASADA): \n(a) to (e) It has been decided that the employees of Bharat Refractories \nLimited (BRL) would be absorbed in Steel Authority of India Limited \n(SAIL) on equivalent scales of pay after the company's ongoing merger \nprocess with SAIL is completed. \nImport of steel \n104. SHRI KAMAL AKHTAR: Will the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n(a) whether it is a fact that India has become a net importer of steel \n\n303 \nover the last few years; \n(b) the figures of import of steel during the last three years; \n(c) the impact of domestic steel prices on the consumers in view of \nthe economic recession globally; and \n(d) by when the price fluctuations would be stabilized? \n†Original notice of the question was received in Hindi. \n\n304 \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF STEEL (SHRI JITIN PRASADA): \n(a) Yes, Sir. India has become a net importer of steel in 2007- 08 and \nthis trend continues in 2008-09. \n(b) The table below shows import of total finished steel in the \ncountry during the last three years and in the current year. \nYear Import of Total Finished  \n Steel (mt) \n2005-06 4.31 \n2006-07 4.93 \n2007-08 7.03 \nApril-Dec. 2008-09* 4.50 \nSOURCE: Joint Plant Committee; * = provisional \n(c) The steel consuming sector of the Indian economy has been \nadversely affected by the meltdown in the global economy and therefore in \nline with the price trends in the international market, the domestic \nsteel prices have also shown a declining trend since October, 2008. \n(d) The prices of commodities like steel are expected to stabilize, \nonce the impact of economic stimulus packages unveiled by various \ncountries, including India, are realized and economic activities like \nconstruction and manufacturing pick up. \nShortage of water for agriculture \n105. SARDAR TARLOCHAN SINGH: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be \npleased to state: \n(a) the water level in Punjab and Haryana and how much it is receding \nevery year? Give the figure of last three years; \n(b) the steps being taken in this regard; and \n(c) how much is the shortage of water for agriculture in both the \nStates? \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI KANTILAL \nBHURIA): (a) The ground water level is being monitored by Central Ground \nWater Board (CGWB) - an organisation of Ministry of Water Resources. The \nposition of water level and declining trend of water level as per the \nCGWB during the last three years in the State of Punjab and Haryana are:- \nPunjab - The depth of water level lies between 0.85 metre below ground \n\n305 \nlevel (m bgl) in Muktsar and 31.32 m bgl in Fatehgarh districts during \npre-monsoon (May) and during post- monsoon (November) varies from 0.84 m \nbgl in Muktsar district to 37.36 m bgl in Ludhiana districts. Very \nshallow water levels, < 2 m bgl i.e. water logging conditions are \nobserved in parts of Ferozpur, Muktsar and in small patches in Gurdaspur \nand Rupnagar districts. Very deep water levels (>20 m) are observed in \nparts of Jalandhar, Kapurthala, Moga, Patiala, Sangrur, Nawanshahar and \nAmritsar districts. \nThe trend of ground water level decline over last three years varies \nfrom less than 10 cm to more than 1 m per year in different parts of the \nState. The water level decline of more than 1 m per year has been \nobserved at a few places in Fatehgarh, Gurdaspur, Hoshiarpur, Jalandhar, \nKapurthala, Ludhiana, Nawanshahr, Patiala, and Sangrur districts. \nHaryana - The depth of water level during May varies from 0.35 m bgl \nin Gurgaon to 47.80 m bgl in Bhiwani district whereas the depth of water \nlevel during November varies from 0.42 m bgl to 57.10 m bgl in Gurgaon \ndistrict. Water logging conditions are observed in the central part of \nthe State covering parts of Bhiwani, Hissar, Jind, Jhajjar, Rohtak and \nSonipat districts and in isolated patches in Gurgaon, Panipat and \nYamunanagar districts. Very deep water levels (> 40 m) occur in Bhiwani \ndistrict bordering Rajasthan. The trend of water level decline over last \nthree years varies from less than 10 cm to more than 2 m per year in \ndifferent parts of the State. Water level decline of more than 1 m per \nyear has been observed at a few places in Ambala, Fatehabad, Gurgaon, \nKaithal, Karnal, Kurukshetra, Panchkula, Panipat, Sirsa and Yamunanagar \ndistricts. \n(b) With a view to prevent water shortage, Government of Punjab has \ntaken the steps like use of Laser Land Leveler, promulgation of 'The \nPunjab Preservation of Sub Soil Water Ordinance, 2008', diversification \nfrom Paddy to less water consuming crops, use of water saving techniques \nand capacity building & Training, etc. \nThe Haryana Government has also taken steps like launching of Mass \nAwareness Programmes for conservation and judicious use of water, \nintroduction of a scheme titled \"Accelerated Recharge to Groundwater\", \nprovision of subsidy on adoption of Drip/Sprinkler System for irrigation \nto prevent over exploitation of groundwater, popularizing growing of less \nwater intensive crops in the areas where water table is declining, etc. \n(c) As per information received from CGWB, Ground water is being over-\n\n306 \nexploited in both the States with rate of ground water development at 145 \n% in Punjab and 109 % in Haryana. The Government of Haryana has reported \nthat requirement of water for agriculture at 62% intensity is about 31.90 \nMillion Acre Feet (MAP), against the total availability from all \nresources of 18.75 MAF. \nOutbreak of Avian Influenza \n106. SHRI MAHENDRA MOHAN: Will the Minister of AGRICULTURE be pleased \nto state: \n(a) whether Government is aware of outbreak of Avian influenza in West \nBengal and North Eastern States recently; \n(b) if so, whether a Central team has been sent to the affected States \nand to ascertain the exact position of the outbreak; \n(c) if so, the details of the report furnished by the Central team; \nand \n(d) the steps taken by Government to check spread of Avian influenza \nin the Country? \n\n307 \n12.00 NOON \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF AGRICULTURE (SHRI \nTASLIMUDDIN): (a) to (c) On the occurrence of Avian Influenza in Assam, \nWest Bengal and Sikkim recently, the Central teams were deputed to aid \nand advise these States in control and containment operations. These \nteams submitted daily reports on the progress of the operations. \n(d) The steps undertaken by the Government to prevent spread of the \ninfection, inter alia, include culling of the entire poultry population \nin the affected zone covering a radius of 3 kilometers from the foci of \ninfection, surveillance of poultry against the disease, training of \nveterinary personnel to tackle Avian Influenza and spreading awareness in \nthe general public on precautions required in handling poultry. \n____________ \nPROCLAMATION UNDER ARTICLE 356 OF THE CONSTITUTION \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HOME AFFAIRS (DR. SHAKEEL \nAHMAD): Sir, I lay on the Table a copy each (in English and Hindi) of the \nfollowing papers: \n (i) Proclamation [G.S.R. No. 33(E)], issued by the President on the \n19th January, 2009, under article 356 of the Constitution in \nrelation to the State of Jharkhand, under clause (3) of the said \narticle. [Placed in Library, See No. L.T. 10445/09] \n (ii) Order [G.S.R. No. 34(E)], dated the 19th January, 2009, made by \nthe President, under sub -clause (i) of clause (c) of the above \nProclamation. [Placed in Library, See No. L.T. 10446/09] \n (iii) Report of the Governor of Jharkhand, dated the 16th January, 2009 \nto the President recommending the issue of the Proclamation. \n[Placed in Library, See No. L.T. 10447/09] \n____________ \nPAPERS LAID ON THE TABLE \nSECRETARY-GENERAL: Sir, I lay on the Table a Statement (in English \nand Hindi) showing the Bills passed by the Houses of Parliament during \nthe Two Hundred and Fourteenth Session of the Rajya Sabha and assented to \nby the President. \n (1) The Appropriation (No. 3) Bill, 2008. \n\n308 \n (2) The Appropriation (Railways) No. 4 Bill, 2008. \n (3) The Indian Maritime University Bill, 2008. \n (4) The National Waterway (Talcher- Dhamra Stretch of Rivers \nGeonkhali-Charbatia Stretch of East Coast Canal, Charbatia-Dhamra \nStretch of Matai River and Mahanadi Delta Rivers) Bill, 2008. \n\n309 \n (5) The National Waterway (Kakinada- Puducherry Stretch of Canals and \nthe Kaluvelly Tank, Bhadrachalam- Rajahmundry Stretch of River \nGodavari and Wazirabad-Vijayawada Stretch of River Krishna) Bill, \n2008. \n (6) The Central Universities Laws (Amendment) Bill, 2008. \n (7) The Drugs and Cosmetics (Amendment) Bill, 2008. \n (8) The Airports Economic Regulatory Authority of India Bill, 2008. \n (9) The President's Emoluments and Pension (Amendment) Bill, 2008. \n (10) The Vice-President's Pension (Amendment) Bill, 2008. \n (11) The Salaries and Allowances of Officers of Parliament (Amendment) \nBill, 2008. \n (12) The Appropriation (No.4) Bill, 2008. \n (13) The Appropriation (Railways) No. 5 Bill, 2008. \n (14) The Unorganized Sector Workers' Social Security Bill, 2008. \n (15) The National Investigation Agency Bill, 2008. \n (16) The Unlawf